{"name":"White Flag","hex":"#c8c2c0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white flag","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flag with a white color, used as a symbol of truce or surrender.","A flag with a white color, signifying the final lap of a race (ie. the bell lap)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_flag"}],"entries":[{"title":"White flag","blocks":[{"text":"White flags have had different meanings throughout history and depending on the locale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/White_flag_waving.svg/330px-White_flag_waving.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_flag"},{"title":"White Flag (band)","blocks":[{"text":"White Flag is an American punk rock band currently based in Los Angeles. Their current lineup consists of frontman Mike Mess (guitar), Jello B. Afro (bass), and Trace Element (drums). These members have been in the band since 1982, though many have rotated in and out. White Flag has a discography that ranges from their 1982 debut R is for Rocket to 2010's Benefit For Cats."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Flag_(band)"},{"title":"White Flag (Dido song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"White Flag\" is a song by English singer-songwriter Dido, released as the lead single from her second studio album, Life for Rent (2003). The song was first released to US radio on 7 July 2003 and was issued in the United Kingdom as a physical single on 1 September 2003. The song performed well on record charts around the world, peaking at number one in Australia, Austria, the Czech Republic, Germany, Italy, Norway, and Portugal. In Dido's native UK, it reached number two on the UK Singles Chart, and in the United States, it climbed to number 18 on the Billboard Hot 100."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/47/White_Flag_Dido.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Flag_(Dido_song)"},{"title":"Flag","blocks":[{"text":"A flag is a piece of fabric with distinctive colours and design, often flown from a pole and used for symbolic, signalling, or decorative purposes. The term flag is also used to refer to the graphic design employed on such pieces of fabric. The study of flags is known as \"vexillology\" from the Latin vexillum, meaning \"flag\" or \"banner\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/United_Nations_Flags_-_cropped.jpg/330px-United_Nations_Flags_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg/500px-Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg/1280px-Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg/500px-Ottoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A white flag displayed during the Ottoman surrender of Jerusalem to Britain, 9 December 1917","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOttoman_surrender_of_Jerusalem_restored.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Villars_a_Denain1.jpg/120px-Villars_a_Denain1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Villars_a_Denain1.jpg/250px-Villars_a_Denain1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"ht